extends layout

block content
  div.flex.flex-col.gap-6.max-w-4xl
    div.card.bg-base-100.shadow
      div.card-body
        h2.card-title On this page
        ul.flex.flex-wrap.gap-x-4.gap-y-1
          li: a.link(href="#start") Getting started
          li: a.link(href="#tenants") Tenants
          li: a.link(href="#roles") Roles
          li: a.link(href="#sql") SQL
          li: a.link(href="#types") Data types
          li: a.link(href="#storage") Table storage
          li: a.link(href="#statistics") Statistics
          li: a.link(href="#maintenance") Merge and checkpoint
          li: a.link(href="#transactions") Transactions
          li: a.link(href="#backups") Backups

    section#start.card.bg-base-100.shadow.scroll-mt-4
      div.card-body
        h2.card-title Getting started
        ul.list-disc.pl-5.space-y-1
          li Dashboard: key figures for every table, and new tables if you are an admin.
          li SQL Console: several statements separated by semicolons. It stops at the first error.
          li Tables: filter with a WHERE condition, sort, page through rows, edit rows and export CSV.
          li Statistics: storage figures for every column of every table.
          li
            | Fiori client: a separate app for browsing and editing rows, at
            a.link.ml-1(href="/ui5/") /ui5/
            | .
        p.text-sm.opacity-70 Tables have no primary key. Editing or deleting a row here affects every row with identical values, and the result message reports how many.

    section#tenants.card.bg-base-100.shadow.scroll-mt-4
      div.card-body
        h2.card-title Tenants
        p You are signed in to tenant #{page.tenant}.
        ul.list-disc.pl-5.space-y-1
          li Each tenant has its own tables, users, backups and backup schedule. Nothing is shared between tenants, and an open transaction only locks its own tenant.
          li Users sign in with the tenant name. Leave the field empty for the default tenant.
          li Admins of a tenant manage the users of that tenant only.
          li System administrators sign in to the tenant system. They create, disable and delete tenants, but they can't see tenant data.
          li Disabling a tenant signs out all of its users. You can delete a tenant only after you disable it, and deleting removes its users, tables and backups for good.

    section#roles.card.bg-base-100.shadow.scroll-mt-4
      div.card-body
        h2.card-title Roles
        p The interface hides what your role can't do. You are signed in as #{page.userName} (#{page.roleLabel}).
        div.overflow-x-auto
          table.table.table-sm
            thead
              tr
                th Role
                th Can do
            tbody
              tr
                td.font-mono viewer
                td SELECT, read tables and statistics
              tr
                td.font-mono editor
                td Also INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE and transactions
              tr
                td.font-mono admin
                td Also CREATE, DROP, merge, checkpoint, users and backups
        p.text-sm.opacity-70 Roles apply to the whole database of your tenant, not to single tables.

    section#sql.card.bg-base-100.shadow.scroll-mt-4
      div.card-body
        h2.card-title SQL
        p.text-sm.font-semibold Tables and rows
        div.mockup-code.text-sm
          pre: code CREATE TABLE orders (id INTEGER, customer VARCHAR, amount NUMERIC)
          pre: code CREATE TABLE small (id INTEGER, name VARCHAR) USING JSON
          pre: code INSERT INTO orders VALUES (1, 'ACME', 99.50)
          pre: code UPDATE orders SET amount = 120.5 WHERE id = 1
          pre: code DELETE FROM orders WHERE customer = 'ACME'
          pre: code DROP TABLE orders
        p.text-sm.font-semibold.mt-2 Queries
        div.mockup-code.text-sm
          pre: code SELECT DISTINCT customer FROM orders ORDER BY customer
          pre: code SELECT customer, COUNT(*), SUM(amount) FROM orders WHERE amount >= 10
          pre: code   GROUP BY customer HAVING COUNT(*) >= 2 ORDER BY SUM(amount) DESC LIMIT 10 OFFSET 20
          pre: code SELECT c.name, o.amount FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id
        ul.list-disc.pl-5.space-y-1
          li Clause order: WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, ORDER BY, LIMIT. OFFSET needs LIMIT.
          li Aggregates: COUNT(*), SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX. SUM and AVG work on numbers only.
          li WHERE supports AND, OR, parentheses, IN, BETWEEN and LIKE, each also with NOT. In LIKE, % matches any characters and _ matches one.
          li JOIN: INNER and LEFT, equality conditions only. A self-join needs two different aliases.
          li Comparisons with NULL are never true, and that includes NOT IN, NOT BETWEEN and NOT LIKE.

    section#types.card.bg-base-100.shadow.scroll-mt-4
      div.card-body
        h2.card-title Data types
        p Column types also accept the synonyms below and the OData name, in any letter case.
        div.overflow-x-auto
          table.table.table-sm
            thead
              tr
                th Type
                th Synonyms
                th Example value
            tbody
              tr
                td.font-mono BOOLEAN
                td.font-mono BOOL
                td.font-mono TRUE
              tr
                td.font-mono TINYINT
                td.font-mono BYTE
                td.font-mono 255
              tr
                td.font-mono SBYTE
                td
                td.font-mono -128
              tr
                td.font-mono SMALLINT
                td.font-mono INT16
                td.font-mono 32767
              tr
                td.font-mono INT32
                td
                td.font-mono 2147483647
              tr
                td.font-mono INTEGER
                td.font-mono INT, BIGINT, INT64
                td.font-mono 42
              tr
                td.font-mono REAL
                td.font-mono SINGLE
                td.font-mono 0.1
              tr
                td.font-mono DECIMAL
                td.font-mono DOUBLE
                td.font-mono 1.5
              tr
                td.font-mono NUMERIC
                td
                td.font-mono 12345678901234567890.55
              tr
                td.font-mono VARCHAR
                td.font-mono NVARCHAR, TEXT
                td.font-mono 'Text'
              tr
                td.font-mono BLOB
                td.font-mono BINARY, VARBINARY
                td.font-mono X'00FF'
              tr
                td.font-mono DATE
                td
                td.font-mono '2026-11-06'
              tr
                td.font-mono TIMESTAMP
                td.font-mono DATETIMEOFFSET
                td.font-mono '2026-11-06T12:30:00+01:00'
              tr
                td.font-mono TIME
                td.font-mono TIMEOFDAY
                td.font-mono '12:30:00'
              tr
                td.font-mono DURATION
                td
                td.font-mono 'P1DT2H'
              tr
                td.font-mono GUID
                td.font-mono UUID
                td.font-mono '01234567-89ab-cdef-0123-456789abcdef'
              tr
                td.font-mono GEO
                td.font-mono GEOMETRY, ST_GEOMETRY
                td.font-mono 'POINT(13.4 52.5)'
              tr
                td.font-mono GEOGRAPHY
                td
                td.font-mono 'POINT(13.4 52.5)'
              tr
                td.font-mono LINK
                td.font-mono URL
                td.font-mono 'https://example.org/a'
        div.alert.alert-warning(role="note")
          i(data-lucide="triangle-alert", class="w-5 h-5")
          span DECIMAL is a floating-point double. Use NUMERIC for exact values such as money.

    section#storage.card.bg-base-100.shadow.scroll-mt-4
      div.card-body
        h2.card-title Table storage
        p Queries always run in memory. Storage only decides how a table survives a restart, and it can't be changed later.
        div.overflow-x-auto
          table.table.table-sm
            thead
              tr
                th Storage
                th Kept in
                th Each change costs
            tbody
              tr
                td.font-mono MEMORY
                td The statement log
                td One log line
              tr
                td.font-mono FILE
                td One tab-separated text file per table
                td Rewriting the whole file
              tr
                td.font-mono JSON
                td One JSON file per table
                td Rewriting the whole file
        p.text-sm.opacity-70 For large or frequently changed tables, MEMORY is the better choice.

    section#statistics.card.bg-base-100.shadow.scroll-mt-4
      div.card-body
        h2.card-title Statistics
        p Each column has a sorted, compressed main store and a small delta store that takes new rows.
        dl.grid.gap-x-6.gap-y-2(class="sm:grid-cols-[12rem_1fr]")
          dt.font-semibold Live rows
          dd Rows that queries can see.
          dt.font-semibold Rows in delta
          dd New or updated rows that aren't merged into the main store yet.
          dt.font-semibold Deleted rows
          dd Rows marked as deleted. Queries skip them, and a merge removes them.
          dt.font-semibold Main and delta distinct
          dd Number of different values in each store. Fewer values compress better.
          dt.font-semibold Bits per value
          dd Size of each value's code in the main store, based on the number of distinct values.
          dt.font-semibold Code size
          dd Memory used by the main store's codes, without dictionaries. It isn't the total memory of the table.

    section#maintenance.card.bg-base-100.shadow.scroll-mt-4
      div.card-body
        h2.card-title Merge and checkpoint
        ul.list-disc.pl-5.space-y-1
          li Delta merge: moves delta rows into the main store. It runs in the background, after startup, from 1000 delta rows on, or from the Dashboard.
          li Deleted rows are removed by a merge. A merge also starts by itself from 1000 deleted rows, if they are more than half of the table.
          li Checkpoint: rewrites the statement log as a compact snapshot. It happens after 1000 statements, or from the Dashboard.

    section#transactions.card.bg-base-100.shadow.scroll-mt-4
      div.card-body
        h2.card-title Transactions
        ul.list-disc.pl-5.space-y-1
          li In the SQL Console, BEGIN stays open across runs until COMMIT or ROLLBACK.
          li An open transaction locks the database for everyone else. Their requests fail at once instead of waiting.
          li After 30 seconds without activity, a transaction rolls back.
          li Savepoints and nested transactions aren't supported.

    section#backups.card.bg-base-100.shadow.scroll-mt-4
      div.card-body
        h2.card-title Backups
        p Admins manage backups on the Backups page, and in the Fiori client.
        ul.list-disc.pl-5.space-y-1
          li A backup contains all tables from every storage type. Users aren't included.
          li By default a backup runs every 24 hours, keeping 7 scheduled and 5 safety backups.
          li Restoring replaces all tables. A safety backup of the current state is saved first.
          li A backup can't be created while a transaction is open.
          li Backups are stored on the same disk as the data and aren't encrypted, so copy them somewhere else too.
